      "predicate": "iadas:authorConclusion",
      "object": "University sports club membership is associated with fewer symptoms of disordered eating and emotional problems. Sports club members had lower body dissatisfaction and higher body appreciation compared to non-members. These results were driven by significant differences between lean-sports members and non-members. Lean-sports membership was associated with the lowest levels of disordered eating symptoms, emotional and conduct problems. Lean sports members had the lowest levels of body dissatisfaction and highest body appreciation."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:limites",
      "object": "First, there is likely to be heterogeneity within sports groupings which we were underpowered to investigate. Similarly, it is possible that there are sex*sports interactions regarding disordered eating symptoms and body image metrics (McDonald et al., 2020), however we were underpowered to investigate these. Second, we lacked information on clinical diagnosis of eating disorders and mental health conditions. We utilised industry standard questionnaire measures that have been validated for use in this population, but these may not be sensitive enough to identify those at slightly lower risk for disordered eating (Papini et al., 2022). Third, while the EAT-26 is a widely used and validated screening tool, it is not a diagnostic instrument. It is possible that the EAT-26 underestimates symptom severity due to underreporting or denial of symptoms, a known issue in clinical and non-clinical populations (Ali et al., 2020; Garner et al., 1982; Papini et al., 2022). As such, the true extent of disordered eating attitudes may be greater than reflected in the self-reported scores. Fourth, we did not measure competition level or physical activity frequency. This is important because both factors may confound the effects of sports membership (McMahon et al., 2017; Picard, 1999). Lastly, this study employed a cross-sectional design and is therefore limited in its ability to make causal inferences. Further work employing longitudinal designs is required."
